# /etc/audit/rules.d/zz-jobbliggaren.rules — gate M-7's detection primitive (#1201).
#
# ONE OWNER, ONE FILE. #1201 owns auditd configuration on this box. A future watch — #197's
# host-secrets directory, say — is added HERE, through this issue's Hemvist, and never as a
# second rules file. A gate with two owners has none, which is the invisibility that created
# #1201 in the first place.
#
# THE FILENAME SORTS LAST BY CONVENTION, NOT BECAUSE A COLLISION WOULD OTHERWISE OCCUR.
# An earlier revision claimed a numeric prefix would be wiped by the `-D` in Debian's own
# audit.rules. That is FALSE, and code-reviewer measured it: augenrules(8) states that "the last
# processed -D directive without an option, if present, is always emitted as the first line in
# the resultant file" — augenrules hoists it, so the ordering collision cannot happen. Confirmed
# on the box 2026-08-10: Debian's rules.d/audit.rules does carry a bare `-D`, and the generated
# /etc/audit/audit.rules carries it as line one. (The same claim also named the wrong algorithm:
# augenrules sorts naturally, `ls -v`, not by C-locale collation.)
#
# `zz-` is kept anyway so ours are the last rules appended, which keeps the generated file easy
# to read and leaves room for future files to be ordered ahead of it deliberately. Never edit
# Debian's audit.rules.
#
# WATCH RULES ONLY, NO SYSCALL RULES. Syscall/execve auditing is auditd's classic
# over-configuration: its volume competes directly with the journal retention floor and with
# ADR 0122's capacity conditions, on a box where the answer to memory pressure (disk swap) is
# forbidden because it breaks gate B-1. These rules emit an event only when something touches a
# watched path.
#
# -D, -b and -f are deliberately ABSENT: Debian's audit.rules owns them and sorts first.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, stated here so nobody reads a tripwire as an alarm. Root can disarm it
# (`auditctl -e 0`), and can erase both the audit log and the journal. A raw device read
# (`dd` against /dev/vda) touches no watched path, and /proc/<pid>/mem touches no file at all —
# so the master key can be taken from a running process without any rule here firing. This is a
# tripwire for the mistake and lower-privilege classes plus a forensic trail. It is not an alarm,
# and it does not lift ADR 0123's scope limit.

# --- The key surface (#198, gate B-1, ADR 0123's asset) -------------------------------------
# The PARENT directory, not .../secrets: a -w against a path that does not exist at load time is
# not loaded — MEASURED 2026-08-10, because this premise is load-bearing and was previously only
# asserted: `auditctl -w /nonexistent/... ` returns "Error sending add rule data request (No such
# file or directory)" and the rule does not appear in `auditctl -l`. The tmpfs subdirectories are
# created by jobbliggaren-tmpfiles.conf, and `audit-rules.service` is
# `After=systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service` — so tmpfiles runs BEFORE this file is loaded, which is
# what makes the parent watch work at all. An earlier revision claimed the opposite ordering; if
# it were true, this rule would abort the whole load on every boot. INSTALL THAT FILE: without it
# nothing recreates /run/jobbliggaren after a reboot, and rule 1 failing takes every other rule
# and auditd with it (measured 2026-08-10: it was absent on this box).
# Watching the parent is recursive and covers both secrets/ and host-secrets/ regardless of
# whether either exists when the rules load. The heartbeat's P4 predicate measures that these
# keys are actually loaded in the kernel, because "the file is on disk" is not the same claim.
-w /run/jobbliggaren -p rwa -k jbl-key-tmpfs

# --- The two controls ADR 0123's open mitigations name ---------------------------------------
# Those mitigations are void as written (measured 2026-08-17; vps-base-hardening.md §11). These
# watches are unaffected: they detect writes to the two controls, which is worth having whether or
# not either control is ever narrowed. Do not read the void as retiring the watches.
# -p wa, not rwa: a read watch on sudoers would fire on every single `sudo` invocation, which is
# alarm fatigue as a mechanism. The attack is the WRITE.
#
# The .ssh DIRECTORY, not the authorized_keys file: OpenSSH's default AuthorizedKeysFile is
# ".ssh/authorized_keys .ssh/authorized_keys2" and nothing in this repo overrides it, so both are
# live and a file watch would cover one of two. Watching the directory also survives the file
# being replaced rather than edited.
#
# /root/.ssh AND NOT /root. A directory watch is recursive (auditctl(8): "almost the same as
# using the -F dir option"), and /root is NOT quiet on this box — measured 2026-08-10: 20 files
# changed in 48 h under /root/.sigstore/root/tuf.db and /root/.docker/buildx, because the hourly
# reconcile runs cosign as root and cosign keeps its TUF cache there. Watching /root would file
# that traffic under jbl-authkeys, turning the one watch that would catch an SSH backdoor into
# the noisiest channel present — the same alarm fatigue this file rejects for sudoers reads.
# /root/.ssh exists (measured the same day), so the narrow watch loads.
-w /home/jpadmin/.ssh -p wa -k jbl-authkeys
-w /root/.ssh         -p wa -k jbl-authkeys
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config    -p wa -k jbl-sshd
-w /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d  -p wa -k jbl-sshd
-w /etc/sudoers            -p wa -k jbl-sudoers
-w /etc/sudoers.d          -p wa -k jbl-sudoers

# Account creation is the same persistence class as an authorized_keys write.
-w /etc/passwd -p wa -k jbl-accounts
-w /etc/shadow -p wa -k jbl-accounts
-w /etc/group  -p wa -k jbl-accounts

# --- Persistence: what installs a backdoor as a unit or a job ---------------------------------
-w /etc/systemd/system              -p wa -k jbl-units
-w /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/systemd -p wa -k jbl-units
# cron has no reader on this box (measured 2026-08-10: no user crontab, only distribution
# entries), so a write here is signal with almost no baseline noise — the cheapest watch present.
-w /etc/crontab            -p wa -k jbl-cron
-w /etc/cron.d             -p wa -k jbl-cron
-w /var/spool/cron/crontabs -p wa -k jbl-cron
# The reconcile unit applies both of these as root every hour; a write here starts arbitrary
# containers with arbitrary mounts. The sibling directory is watched above, these were not.
-w /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/docker-compose.yml -p wa -k jbl-deploy
-w /opt/jobbliggaren/deploy/.env               -p wa -k jbl-deploy
# The heartbeat's own credential. Reading it is the one action that defeats the dead-man (see
# jobbliggaren-heartbeat.sh's header), so -p r is deliberate here where it is avoided elsewhere:
# this file has no legitimate reader except the heartbeat itself, so the baseline is knowable.
-w /etc/jobbliggaren/detection.env -p rwa -k jbl-detection

# --- The detection configuration itself -------------------------------------------------------
-w /etc/audit -p wa -k jbl-auditconf

# --- Enable, mutable (-e 1). THE RATCHET TO -e 2 IS A CONDITION, NOT A DATE. ------------------
# -e 2 makes the rule set immutable until reboot and refuses `auditctl -e 0` — the named
# disarm vector. It is not v1 for a measured reason: on this box every rule change would then
# cost a reboot AND a manual key re-injection (the master key is RAM-only, #198), and the
# baseline noise is not measured yet. An immutable rule set that is WRONG is worse than a mutable
# one that is right. -e 2 also closes only one vector: `rm -rf /var/log/audit` is untouched by it.
#
# RATCHET CONDITION, with its instrument: once the baseline-noise list in
# docs/runbooks/host-detection.md has held over a written window of at least 14 days (measured
# with `ausearch -if /var/log/audit/audit.log -k jbl-key-tmpfs` — the bare `-k` form returns
# nothing on this box while the events exist, so it would report a silent baseline and invite the
# flip on an empty measurement), flip this to `-e 2` at the next
# PLANNED reboot — which already requires key injection, so the marginal cost is zero. Do not
# schedule an outage for the ratchet's sake.
-e 1
